          ],
          "text": "This 28-year-old male was admitted with an 18-hour history of right iliac fossa pain, nausea, vomiting and anorexia, and was diagnosed with acute appendicitis.",
          "unverified_fact_ids": []
        },
        {
          "section_id": "sections.12",
          "part": "part_b",
          "heading": "admission_summary",
          "fact_ids": [
            "key_investigations.0",
            "key_investigations.1",
            "key_investigations.2",
            "treatment.0",
            "treatment.1",
            "treatment.2"
          ],
          "text": "Investigations showed a white cell count of 14.2 and CRP of 68; urine dipstick was negative. Ultrasound demonstrated free fluid in the right iliac fossa with the appendix not well visualised. He was managed with nil by mouth, IV fluids and IV co-amoxiclav, and proceeded to laparoscopic appendicectomy. Intraoperative findings confirmed an acutely inflamed appendix with no perforation; the procedure was uncomplicated with minimal estimated blood loss. Absorbable sutures were used and do not require removal.",
          "unverified_fact_ids": []
        },
        {
          "section_id": "sections.13",
          "part": "part_b",
